# 216. Boundary Traversal of Binary Tree

## APPROACH: BOUNDARY TRAVERSAL (ANTI-CLOCKWISE 3-STEP DFS)

Idea:
Boundary traversal root se start hoke anti-clockwise direction me 4 parts me divide hoti hai:
1. Root Node (agar wo leaf na ho).
2. Left Boundary nodes (top to bottom, excluding leaves).
3. Saari Leaf Nodes (left to right order me).
4. Right Boundary nodes (bottom to top / reverse order, excluding leaves).

Leaf nodes ko boundary functions me include nahi karte taaki leaves do baar duplicate add na hon.

--------------------------------------------------

APPROACH

1. Edge Case:
   Agar `root == null` ho, empty list return karo.

2. Root Handling:
   Agar `root` akela leaf node nahi hai (`!isLeaf(root)`), toh usse result me add karo.

3. Step 1: Left Boundary (`addLeftBoundary(root.left)`):
   - Loop chalao jab tak node null na ho.
   - Agar current node leaf nahi hai, toh result me add karo.
   - Left child exist karta hai toh `curr = curr.left`, warna `curr = curr.right`.

4. Step 2: Leaf Nodes (`addLeaves(root)`):
   - Simple Preorder / DFS traversal karo.
   - Jab bhi koi leaf node mile (`node.left == null && node.right == null`), uski value result me add karo.

5. Step 3: Right Boundary (`addRightBoundary(root.right)`):
   - Loop chalao jab tak node null na ho.
   - Agar current node leaf nahi hai, toh usse ek temporary list/stack me add karo.
   - Right child exist karta hai toh `curr = curr.right`, warna `curr = curr.left`.
   - Temporary list ko reverse karke result list me add karo (Bottom to Top order ke liye).

--------------------------------------------------

EXAMPLE

Tree:
          5
        /   \
       1     2
      /     / \
     8     4   5
      \
       6

Dry Run:
- Root: [5]
- Left Boundary (excluding leaf):
  - Node 1 (not leaf) -> add 1
  - Node 8 (has right child 6, not leaf) -> add 8
  - Node 6 is leaf -> stop
  - Ans so far: [5, 1, 8]
- Leaves (Left to Right):
  - Traversal visits: 6, 4, 5
  - Ans so far: [5, 1, 8, 6, 4, 5]
- Right Boundary (Reverse order, excluding leaf):
  - Node 2 (not leaf) -> add to temp
  - Node 5 is leaf -> stop
  - Reverse temp: [2]
- Final Ans: [5, 1, 8, 6, 4, 5, 2]

--------------------------------------------------

WHY SEPARATE LEAF LOGIC?

- Agar left aur right boundary me leaves ko bhi include kar lete, toh corner wali leaf nodes (jaise leftmost aur rightmost leaf) do-do baar add ho jaati.
- Isliye rule hai:
  - Left boundary: Non-leaf only
  - Leaves function: All leaves
  - Right boundary: Non-leaf only

--------------------------------------------------

COMPLEXITY

N = Total number of nodes in tree
H = Height of binary tree

Time:
O(N) ->
- Left boundary: O(H)
- Right boundary: O(H)
- Leaves traversal: O(N) (har node visit hoti hai)
Total Time = O(N).

Space:
O(H) -> Recursive stack for leaves + temp list for right boundary (at most height H).
Worst case (skewed tree): O(N)
Best/Average case (balanced tree): O(log N)
*(Excluding output list ans)*

--------------------------------------------------

INTERVIEW POINT

- Traversal Direction Rule:
  - Left boundary upar se neeche jaati hai (Top to Bottom).
  - Right boundary neeche se upar aati hai (Bottom to Top), isliye Stack ya temporary list ko reverse karna zaroori hai.
- Navigation Rule:
  - Left boundary me hamesha left jao; agar left null hai tabhi right jao.
  - Right boundary me hamesha right jao; agar right null hai tabhi left jao.
- Edge case: Single node tree (`root` jisme koi child na ho) -> ans sirf `[root.data]` hona chahiye, duplicate add nahi hona chahiye.

--------------------------------------------------

# CODE:

import java.util.*;

/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * public class TreeNode {
 *     int data;
 *     TreeNode left;
 *     TreeNode right;
 *     TreeNode(int val) { data = val; left = null; right = null; }
 * }
 **/

class Solution {
    public List<Integer> boundary(TreeNode root) {
        List<Integer> ans = new ArrayList<>();
        if (root == null) return ans;

        // Root leaf nahi hai toh add karo (leaf function isse baad me cover karega)
        if (!isLeaf(root)) {
            ans.add(root.data);
        }

        // 1. Left boundary top-to-bottom
        addLeftBoundary(root.left, ans);

        // 2. All leaves left-to-right
        addLeaves(root, ans);

        // 3. Right boundary bottom-to-top (reverse)
        addRightBoundary(root.right, ans);

        return ans;
    }

    private boolean isLeaf(TreeNode node) {
        return (node.left == null && node.right == null);
    }

    private void addLeftBoundary(TreeNode node, List<Integer> ans) {
        TreeNode curr = node;
        while (curr != null) {
            if (!isLeaf(curr)) ans.add(curr.data);
            if (curr.left != null) curr = curr.left;
            else curr = curr.right;
        }
    }

    private void addLeaves(TreeNode node, List<Integer> ans) {
        if (node == null) return;
        if (isLeaf(node)) {
            ans.add(node.data);
            return;
        }

        addLeaves(node.left, ans);
        addLeaves(node.right, ans);
    }

    private void addRightBoundary(TreeNode node, List<Integer> ans) {
        TreeNode curr = node;
        List<Integer> temp = new ArrayList<>();

        while (curr != null) {
            if (!isLeaf(curr)) temp.add(curr.data);
            if (curr.right != null) curr = curr.right;
            else curr = curr.left;
        }

        // Reverse order me add karo bottom-to-top ke liye
        for (int i = temp.size() - 1; i >= 0; i--) {
            ans.add(temp.get(i));
        }
    }
}
